A||N||4||chester||Duke fuel pump - should it run with ignition on.||||5||
Z||000000||chester||07-21-2001||04:09 PM||||I finally got around to messing with my 86 T-top coupe. I've been trying to get the old gas out of the tank. I disconnected the fuel line from the throttle body in hopes of pumping the fuel out of the tank.  To my surprise I found that the fuel pump only runs when I'm cranking the car.  I get no flow when the ignition switch is in the ON position.  I'm assuming that this is a problem. Is the fuel pump on the duke supposed to run with the ignition switch in the ON position? If so what can be the cause of it only running while the car is being cranked? Thanks in advance!<P>  Rob D.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||5
Z||000001||Santa Cruzer||07-21-2001||04:26 PM||||yes, as soon as you turn the ignition on it should run..yourt best bet is to get a trip permit $15 and run all the gas out of it then fill it up with high test..||||reg||12
Z||000002||88CoupeV6||07-21-2001||05:13 PM||||Yep, It should run for 10 sec. It is to build pressure. May be a relay. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||07-21-2001||06:37 PM||||The pump should run with the key on. From Haynes Manual:"when key is first turned on,the ECM will turn on fuel pump relay for 2 seconds. If the engine is not started within two seconds, the ECM will shut off the fuel pump until engine is started."  The oil press switch will energize the relay if cranking is continued and oil press reaches about 4psi. However, it also states a little further down that the ECM will turn on the fuel pump and keep it running as long as the engine is cranking and ECM is receiving HEI distributor<BR>pulses. So it probably is not possible to use the fuel pump to drain the tank with just the key on, unless you 'jumper or hotwire' the fuel pump relay. I've  never done this so won't say how I think you should proceed. I think I would not drive to run out the gas if you think it is bad. Might sludge up something. I can email you the fp wiring diagram if you need it,already have it on hard drive from another post. If anyone else finds error in this explanation, please feel free to correct me.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||07-21-2001||06:58 PM||||Don't know about fiero's but on other older GM fuel injected cars the fuel pump electrical circuit is wired in series with the oil pressure switch. If the fuel pump relay fails the oil switch will close the fuel pump circuit as soon as oil pressure builds. This condition would cause long cranking times if the car was running, the fuel pump wont turn on until the starter builds enough oil pressure to close the oil pressure switch with a failed relay.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
